299 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 179 0 R
/E (Currently, I am interested in pursuing a career in small animal muscular rehabilitation, and possibly orthopedics. I am also interested in wildlife and equine medicine. I am fascinated by the musculoskeletal system, and therapies used to help animals rebuild muscular strength. I believe this interest of mine developed because I grew up as a competitive Scottish highland dancer. As a dancer I was constantly learning about how the body moves, and how to strengthen and stretch muscles appropriately to achieve goals and avoid injury. I taught dance classes throughout high school, which made me fall in love with taking on a leadership role, and inspired me towards becoming a veterinarian.)
/Pg 14 0 R
/K [7]
>>
endobj
300 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 179 0 R
/E (Throughout undergrad, I started working as a veterinary assistant for a small animal clinic in Guelph, and back home. For both of these experiences I started out as a volunteer, and worked my way up to a paid position. I absolutely loved working in a clinic and getting to learn directly from the veterinarians. While working at the clinic at home, I got to observe multiple canine orthopedic surgeries. The surgeon talked me through each surgery, allowing me to learn and see what it is like to be a specialty surgeon. Some of my favorite campus opportunities from undergrad include College Royal, working with the OAC Liaison Team and taking the Experiments in Animal Biology class in my final semester. In this class, I got to conduct a research experiment with a group of my peers \(including one of my best friends!\) and work directly with piglets. )
/Pg 14 0 R
/K [8]
>>
endobj
301 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 179 0 R
/E (Since graduation, I have been using my gap year to gain a variety of experiences to explore more fields in veterinary medicine, learn more about myself, travel and learn new skills! From May to February, I continued to work as a veterinary assistant and worked as a supervisor at a pet boarding facility. Alongside work, I started volunteering at an equine barn, and with a local not-for-profit that provides accessible & low-cost vaccines and examinations for pets in the community. After saving up for a few months, I hopped on a plane in February to Australia! During my travels, I have been volunteering at a wildlife hospital & rehabilitation facility, where I help with the daily weighing, feeding and medication administration of sick and injured wildlife, helping them recover prior to release. I have had the pleasure to work with so many species, including exotic birds, small marsupials, lizards, kangaroos and more! I hope to continue travelling throughout my veterinary career!)
/Pg 14 0 R
/K [9]
>>
endobj
